{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '320,370p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md | sed -n '260,310p'\"","aggregated_output":"   320\t### 3.1 WebSocket\n   321\t\n   322\t`dao.stream.ws` as specified in `dao.stream.ws.md` is the socket layer, and\n   323\ta channel is one of its attachments plus a projection. **Reused**: the\n   324\tattachment model and identity; the adapter that deposits every event as\n   325\t`{:ws/attachment id :ws/event e :ws/value v}` on the medium the composition\n   326\twired; the writer handle, whose `append!` frames each value as one\n   327\t`{:ws/frame :ws/value :ws/value v}` message in the negotiated codec profile\n   328\t(Transit-JSON text or the CBOR binary profile); the bounded acceptance\n   329\thandoff with slot and acknowledgement; the resolution and lifecycle\n   330\tvocabulary. Ordered and reliable; frame budget unbounded.\n   331\t\n   332\t**New, the projection.** The mirror and the link speak raw request and\n   333\tanswer maps, so each channel end composes one step, `ws-project`, between\n   334\tthe ws deposit medium and the channel. The channel reader is a ring buffer\n   335\tthe composition wires; the channel writer is the ws handle itself.\n   336\t`ws-project` holds the reading cursor on the attachment's traffic medium,\n   337\tkeeps the events whose `:ws/attachment` names this channel, appends the\n   338\t`:ws/value` of each `:ws/payload` onto the ring buffer, and drops\n   339\t`:ws/error` diagnostics. A terminal lifecycle event for the attachment\n   340\t(`:ws/closed`, `:ws/ended`) or a failure resolution (`:ws/not-found`,\n   341\t`:ws/transport-error`) makes `ws-project` close the ring buffer: channel\n   342\tloss is then the link's `end` observation (2.4). `ws-project` is a step the\n   343\tcomposition drives at its own cadence, as it drives `endpoint-step`.\n   344\t\n   345\tDirection is establishment, not authority. A WebSocket has a dialer and an\n   346\tacceptor because TCP does. Once established the channel is symmetric and\n   347\tnothing in this document remembers who dialed. Hosts: clj, cljs on Node and\n   348\tcljd may dial or accept (`src/clj/dao/stream/ws/jvm.clj`,\n   349\t`src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/node.cljs`, `src/cljd/dao/stream/ws/dart.cljd`);\n   350\tcljs in a browser may only dial (`src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/browser.cljs`).\n   351\t\n   352\t### 3.2 UDP\n   353\t\n   354\t`dao.stream.udp` is a new channel with the WebSocket deposit model: a socket\n   355\tretains nothing, so it has no reader surface, and its adapter deposits every\n   356\tdatagram as an event carrying the datagram's source address and its decoded\n   357\tvalue onto the medium the composition wired.\n   358\t\n   359\t- Descriptor: `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :dao.stream/identity\n   360\t  <channel identity> :dao.stream.udp/host h :dao.stream.udp/port p}`.\n   361\t  `attach!` binds or reuses a local socket and returns a handle whose writer\n   362\t  path is datagrams to that address.\n   363\t- One CBOR value per datagram, canonical profile. The budget covers the\n   364\t  whole encoded datagram, fragment envelope included: at most 1200 bytes,\n   365\t  the figure `dao.jing.dht.node` already uses to clear common path MTUs.\n   366\t- **Fragmentation is channel-internal.** An encoded message over budget is\n   367\t  split into `k` datagrams, each\n   368\t  `{:dao.stream.remote/id n :dao.stream.udp/part i :dao.stream.udp/parts k\n   369\t  :dao.stream.udp/bytes b}`, where `n` is the carried message's own request\n   370\t  id. The receiver reassembles by `[channel attachment identity,\n   260\t :ws/admission  {:retention :evict-oldest\n   261\t                 :capacity <positive-integer>\n   262\t                 :value-domain :host-values}}\n   263\t```\n   264\t\n   265\tto that slot's acknowledgement medium. The composition creates this\n   266\tper-attachment traffic medium and mints its `:dao.stream/newest` cursor before\n   267\tdepositing the acknowledgement, retaining the reader and cursor in session\n   268\tstate. The transport validates the writer and admission declaration before\n   269\tenabling delivery. A wrong-identity acknowledgement is stale and ignored\n   270\twithout changing the outstanding slot. A matching acknowledgement with a\n   271\tmalformed writer, surface, or admission declaration is rejected:\n   272\t`endpoint-step` releases the slot, closes the pending connection before\n   273\tacceptance, and its close-completion deposits `:ws/closed` on the control\n   274\tmedium.\n   275\t\n   276\tEvery event before acceptance is enabled — including decode diagnostics,\n   277\tresolution, and the terminal event for pre-accept teardown — lands in the\n   278\tboundary control medium. Every payload, diagnostic, and terminal event after\n   279\tacceptance is enabled lands in that attachment's acknowledged traffic medium.\n   280\tThe serving composition reads both: the control medium retires a pending\n   281\toffer/session, while the per-attachment medium retires an established session.\n   282\t\n   283\tThe transport exports an explicit\n   284\t`endpoint-step` operation. The serving composition's single driver calls it\n   285\twith endpoint state and an explicit `now`; the step accepts only an\n   286\tacknowledgement whose identity matches the slot's outstanding offer, sends the\n   287\twire `:ws/accept` frame, enables value delivery, and releases the slot for\n   288\treuse. It never schedules itself. Admission expiry is a constructor policy\n   289\texpressed in the same clock domain as `now`, and `nil` means no expiry. Stale\n   290\tacknowledgements are ignored; attachment identities are not reused during the\n   291\tboundary lifetime.\n   292\t\n   293\tAn outstanding accepted offer cannot be evicted: it is the slot's sole\n   294\tvalue, and the transport does not append another offer to that slot before\n   295\tacknowledgement. Reuse may later evict the acknowledged offer, but by then\n   296\tthe composition has both read the event and retained its handle. A reader\n   297\tlagging before acknowledgement prevents slot reuse rather than losing the\n   298\tcapability. Thus eviction of an unacknowledged acceptance event is\n   299\timpossible by construction.\n   300\t\n   301\tThe handoff remains bounded. When no slot is free, the transport admits no\n   302\tadditional connection; it closes the new socket before logical acceptance,\n   303\tand the client resolves it as `:ws/transport-error`. It neither overwrites\n   304\tan outstanding offer nor grows an unbounded pending queue.\n   305\t\n   306\tOwnership remains explicit in every failure case:\n   307\t\n   308\t- if the offer deposit is non-`ok`, the endpoint closes the pending\n   309\t  connection;\n   310\t- before acknowledgement, the endpoint retains the handle and can close it\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
